# 🚀 DVC (Data Version Control) Tutorial - DagsHub Edition

DVC acts just like Git, but for your large data files. Instead of storing massive CSVs in GitHub, DVC stores them in a remote cloud server. 

Since Google Drive has extremely strict security blocking, we will use **DagsHub**. DagsHub is like GitHub for Machine Learning—it gives you a completely free, out-of-the-box DVC server with zero configuration!

Here is the exact step-by-step setup for both the Machine Learning Engineer (You) and the Data Labeler (Collaborator).

---

## Part 1: The ML Engineer (You) - Initial Setup 
*You only need to do this once to set up the project.*

### 1. Create a DagsHub Project
1. Go to [dagshub.com](https://dagshub.com/) and create a free account (you can sign in with GitHub).
2. Click **Create Project > Connect a Repository**. Connect this GitHub repository.
3. Click the green **Remote** button at the top right of your DagsHub repo, click **DVC**, and copy the URL provided (it looks like `https://dagshub.com/<username>/<repo>.dvc`).

### 2. Initialize DVC & Configure DagsHub
In your terminal (make sure your `.venv` is active):
```powershell
# You don't need dvc[gdrive] anymore, just standard DVC!
dvc init
git commit -m "Initialize DVC"

# Tell DVC to use your DagsHub server (replace the URL with yours!)
dvc remote add origin "https://dagshub.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_REPO.dvc"
dvc remote modify origin --local auth basic
dvc remote modify origin --local user "YOUR_USERNAME"
dvc remote modify origin --local password "YOUR_DAGSHUB_TOKEN"

# Commit the configuration to Git
git add .dvc/config
git commit -m "Configure DagsHub as DVC remote"
```
*(Note: Because we used the `--local` flag, your password/token is saved in `.dvc/config.local` and will **never** be pushed to GitHub!)*

### 3. Track and Push Initial Data
```powershell
# Tell DVC to track the data folder
dvc add backend/AI/data

# Commit the pointer file to Git
git add backend/AI/data.dvc backend/AI/.gitignore
git commit -m "Track AI dataset using DVC"

# Upload the actual heavy data files to DagsHub
dvc push -r origin
```
*(Now the dataset is safely in DagsHub, and Git is tracking the `.dvc` pointer! Push your Git commits to GitHub so your collaborators can see them.)*

